<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="1.3.6.1.4.1.19376.1.5.3.1.3.1" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="42349-1" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Reason for referral (narrative)"/>
    <title>Reason for Referral</title>
    <text><paragraph>Type 2 diabetes above goal in a 71-year-old man with autonomic and peripheral neuropathy and orthostatic hypotension. Please confirm whether diabetic autonomic neuropathy explains his lightheadedness on standing, advise on treating it, and help choose add-on glucose-lowering therapy. [S]</paragraph><paragraph>Urgency: Routine, within four weeks; to coincide with his diabetes check. [S]</paragraph><paragraph>Questions for the consultant:</paragraph><list listType="ordered"><item>Is diabetic autonomic neuropathy a sufficient explanation for his orthostatic hypotension, and should the earlier Shy-Drager label be carried forward?</item><item>If the non-drug measures are not enough, which medicine do you recommend, and how should we watch for high pressure lying down?</item><item>Which add-on glucose-lowering agent is safest, given his risk of unrecognized low glucose, his falls, and his orthostatic hypotension?</item><item>Do you agree with an HbA1c goal below 7.5 percent?</item><item>Should his seated blood pressure above 130/80 be treated?</item></list></text>

  </section>
</component>

<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="1.3.6.1.4.1.19376.1.5.3.1.3.4"/>
    <code code="10164-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="History of Present illness Narrative"/>
    <title>History of Present Illness</title>
    <text><paragraph>Mr. Cash is seen at the request of Dr. Hale, on the same day as his four-week diabetes check. Since the first visit he has taken metformin 1000 mg twice daily without stomach upset, and his fasting home glucose readings run 140 to 170 mg/dL, with none below 70 and no shakiness, sweating, or confusion. He stands up in stages, has raised the head of his bed, wears waist-high compression, and drinks about 2 liters a day. He is still lightheaded on standing most mornings and after meals, but he has had no further falls and no fainting. He has no nausea, diarrhea, early fullness, or difficulty passing urine. His feet are numb to the ankles, unchanged. He asks what is causing the lightheadedness. [S]</paragraph><paragraph>Historical basis. These claims are historical evidence, not findings from a modern examination.</paragraph><list><item>J. R. (Johnny) Cash was born on February 26, 1932, in Kingsland, Cleveland County, Arkansas, to Ray and Carrie Cash. [D]</item><item>Cash&apos;s older brother Jack died in a sawmill accident in 1944. [D]</item><item>In 1957 Cash began taking amphetamines to stay awake on a long road trip, the start of an addiction that lasted about a decade; in a 2002 interview he named amphetamines and barbiturates in the 1960s. [D]</item><item>Cash was hospitalized in 1983 with internal bleeding; after recovering he entered the Betty Ford Clinic for treatment. [D]</item><item>In October 1997 Cash was diagnosed with Shy-Drager syndrome, an illness similar to Parkinson&apos;s disease; in a 2000 interview he said the diagnosis was a misdiagnosis. [D]</item><item>Cash later learned he had autonomic neuropathy; in November 2002 he described it as a deadening of the nerve endings in his lower extremities that had taken the strength from his legs, so that he could walk, but not very well. [D]</item><item>Cash was repeatedly hospitalized for pneumonia in his last years; he was treated for pneumonia at Baptist Hospital in Nashville in February 2001, and in November 2002 he said he had had pneumonia four times in three years. [D]</item><item>In November 2002 Cash said about 60 percent of his vision was gone, which he attributed to the neuropathy and the diabetes, and he said he hoped the glaucoma could also be reversed. [D]</item></list><paragraph>1 later historical claim is omitted because it describes events after the index age. See the teaching note.</paragraph></text>

  </section>
</component>

